course: dictionary
lesson: 6053
title: Menthol: a cool feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name something you can feel, smell or taste. Menthol is one of them. The dictionary says it is a substance from mint oil that gives a cool, fresh feeling and smell.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Menthol is found in cough sweets, chest rubs, toothpaste and chewing gum. These things often taste or smell fresh and minty, so menthol is the reason they feel cool.
explain: The example sentence shows something surprising: «Menthol in mint switches on the nerve sensors that detect cold, so the mouth feels cool though it is not colder.» The mouth feels cool, but its temperature has not changed. A feeling and a fact are not always the same thing.
fact: Menthol is a substance that comes from mint oil.
fact: Menthol gives a cool, fresh feeling and smell.
fact: You can find menthol in cough sweets, chest rubs, toothpaste and chewing gum.
fact: Menthol is a noun, because it names a substance.
fact: «Menthol» makes the mouth feel cool, though it is not colder.
exercise: «Menthol in mint gives a cool, fresh feeling.» What is menthol? | *A substance from mint oil | A hot drink | A small bird that lives in mint fields | A kind of cold weather
exercise: Which thing often contains menthol? | A bag of crisps | *A cough sweet | A glass of milk | A ripe banana
exercise: «...so the mouth feels cool though it is not colder.» Why does the mouth feel cool? | The mouth has gone colder | It is raining inside the mouth | It has been put in a fridge | *Menthol switches on the nerve sensors that detect cold
test: Menthol gives a feeling that is | *A cool, fresh feeling and smell | A hot, spicy feeling | A sour taste | A loud noise
test: Which word names a substance? | Feeling | Fresh | *Menthol | Cool
exam: Menthol is in cough sweets, chest rubs, toothpaste and chewing gum. Which of these is NOT on that list? | Toothpaste | Chewing gum | *Hot chocolate | Chest rub
